AU  - Lurie, Fedor
TI  - Transforming the best care into the standard for care.
PY  - 2016
AB  - Serving the American Venous Forum (AVF) as its President is a distinct honor and one of the greatest privileges an individual can have as a professional. This position comes with responsibilities but also benefits, such as being able to deliver the presidential address. In the time of fast-paced scientific meetings, when even a 10-minute presentation seems to be too long, having 45 minutes to talk on issues that are important from your own perspective is a lifetime opportunity. The opportunity to publish this address is a mixed blessing, as this paper most likely will never be cited and will completely ruin your personal citation index. Being cited, however, is not the purpose of the address. Listening to the presidential address, one realizes that each president selects the most significant topic of the year. By discussing these topics publically, the presidents give us, the members of this Society, insight into their thought processes and decision-making. They present their views on the history of this Society, its position in the field of our specialty, and directions for the future. Such insights give each member of the Forum a chance to influence this direction by selecting our future leaders or becoming one of them. I shall follow our tradition and use this opportunity to share with you my views on the current moment, the challenges that we face, and the role of the AVF in the field of venous and lymphatic disorders today. I shall start from 1987, which was a remarkable year. A record number of papers directly related to treatment of chronic venous disease were published: 26 in total, all in
